course: dictionary
lesson: 8577
title: A scholar
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A scholar is a person who studies one subject very deeply and knows a great deal about it. «In Baghdad's House of Wisdom scholars translated Greek, Persian and Indian books into Arabic.» The clue is the books: scholars are people who study and work with learned books.
explain: The word can also mean a pupil who has won a scholarship, which is money to pay for studies. «A school may call its prize-winning pupils scholars.» Context tells you which meaning fits: a scholar of history is an expert, not a child at a desk.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «Ideas travel like a football passed down a long line of players: the digits were passed on by scholars writing in Arabic.» Does «people who know a great deal» fit there? Yes, so you know you have the right meaning.
fact: A scholar is an expert who studies one subject very deeply, such as history or old languages.
fact: A scholar can also be a pupil who has won a scholarship, which is money to pay for studies.
fact: Old-fashioned, informal use: a scholar is any pupil or schoolchild, or someone good at learning.
fact: «No scholar» means not good at book learning.
fact: «Scholars» in Baghdad translated Greek, Persian and Indian books into Arabic, so many Greek works survived.
exercise: «In Baghdad's House of Wisdom scholars translated Greek, Persian and Indian books into Arabic.» What is a scholar here? | *A person who studies deeply and knows a great deal | A shop worker who sells books | A river boat | A fast runner
exercise: «The digits were passed on by scholars writing in Arabic.» Which clue shows the scholars were learned people? | They were playing a game in the park | *They were writing ideas about maths and learning in Arabic | They were eating their lunch | They were sailing across the sea
exercise: Mia says a scholar is always a child at school. Is she right? | Yes, every scholar wears a school uniform | Yes, scholars are always scared of books | *Not always: a scholar can be an expert, or a pupil who has won a scholarship | No, scholars never go near books
test: «He was no scholar, so the old book was hard for him to read.» What does «no scholar» suggest? | *Not good at book learning | Very wise and famous | A top expert on history | A pupil who won money for school
test: A school calls its prize-winning pupils «scholars». What is a scholarship? | A lesson about the Bible | *Money to pay for a pupil's studies | A school trip to the seaside | A type of old book
exam: Which sentence uses «scholars» to mean experts who study old books and languages? | *In history, the scholars studied old books and languages | The scholars ate their lunch in the playground | The scholars wore new trainers to school | The scholars won a race on sports day
